title: HTML containers
subtitle: indentation as structure
members:
  - { name: Ada Lovelace, role: analyst,  lead: true  }
  - { name: Grace Hopper, role: admiral,  lead: true  }
  - { name: Alan Turing,  role: cryptanalyst, lead: false }
+++
<header class="page"
  # {{ self.title }}
  _{{ self.subtitle }}_

A line that is only an opening tag — no closing `>` — opens a container, and
everything indented two spaces past it is its content. The closing tag is
never written: the end of the indent is the end of the element.

<div class="callout"
  ## Ordinary markdown, ordinarily processed

  The two spaces are stripped before anything parses, so this is just a
  paragraph, and the list below is just a list:

  - **bold** and `code` work
  - so do [links](https://example.com)

  > [!NOTE]
  > Alerts, tables, and fenced code all work in here too — a fence just
  > carries the container's indent with it.
This paragraph is outside the callout: the indent ended, so the element did.

## Nesting, and generated content

Containers nest — each level strips its own two spaces — and because the
content is just indented markdown, a template writes the indent itself.

A container holds **block** content, so give it a block element to be — a
`<div`/`<section` around markdown, not a `<ul` around list items (that would
nest a real `<ul>` inside yours) and not a `<p` (which would nest a paragraph
inside a paragraph).

<section class="team"
  <div class="roster"
{% for (const m of self.members) { %}
    - **{{ m.name }}** — {{ m.role }}{{ m.lead ? ' _(lead)_' : '' }}
{% } %}

  <div class="count"
    {{ self.members.length }} people, {{ self.members.filter((m) => m.lead).length }} of them leads.

## Nesting another document

`$.render` returns a whole multi-line block at column zero, which would end a
container rather than fill it. Its optional indent is the fix: the number of
spaces to put on the front of every returned line. Here the card document is
rendered four deep — two for the `<section`, two more for the `<article`.

<section class="roster-cards"
{% for (const m of self.members) { %}
  <article class="card"
{{ $.render({ template: 'card' }, m, 4) }}
{% } %}

## Element kinds

Void elements stand alone; they never get a closing tag:

<hr class="rule"

<img src="team.png" alt="The team"

Raw-text elements keep their body exactly as written — de-indented, but never
touched by markdown:

<pre class="ascii"
     _____
    /     \    *not* emphasis
   |  o o  |   and    the    spacing    survives
    \_____/

<style
  .callout { border-left: 3px solid #ccc; padding-left: 1rem }

## What is *not* a container

A line ending in `>` is an ordinary raw HTML block, exactly as before — so
existing hand-written HTML keeps its meaning, indentation and all:

<div class="untouched">
  <p>This is passed straight through, closing tag and all.</p>
</div>

And an opener shown inside a fence is sample text, not structure:

```md
<div class="example"
  This block is displayed, not expanded.
```
---
template: card
+++
### {{ arg.name }}

{{ arg.role }}{{ arg.lead ? ' — team lead' : '' }}
